-----------------------------------------------------------------------
WASM32 backend status (this fork)
---------------------------------

This fork's `wasm32-gen.c` targets WebAssembly (core 1.0, no bulk-memory
beyond a `memory.copy` import shim) and is validated against the upstream
tests2 corpus via `tests/wasm-corpus/run-corpus.mjs` (the runner's JS libc
in `/root/src/sh2runtime/src/c-runtime.js` provides the `env.$*` imports).

Current status (2026-08-15, mob@2b5a9e6): 111/122 PASS, 9 NO-EXPECT
(companion files the corpus does not score), 1 WRONG, 1 REFUSE, 0
COMPILE-OUT, 0 RUN-CRASH, 0 RUN-TIMEOUT.

The wasm32 codegen model
------------------------

- every function body is a pc-dispatch: `local.get $pc; br_table` over
  nested pad blocks.  A `WasmLabel`/`WasmEdge`/`WasmSeg` graph is laid
  out per function (`w_layout`) into those subs; jumps set `$pc` and
  `br` back to the dispatcher.
- structs are passed by value via caller-side parking temps (the callee
  receives a pointer); the whole parking depth is reserved by lowering
  sp so the callee's frame cannot clobber the temps.
- function pointers use a funcref table (`__indirect_function_table`,
  slot 0 = NULL): a function's address resolves to its table slot, and
  an indirect call pops the slot and emits `call_indirect` with a
  patchable type index.  Address-taken externs (global fn-ptr
  initializers, `&extern_fn` args) are registered as env imports with
  the per-call signature; the element section matches the import's
  signature to the call that pops the slot.
- computed gotos: `&&label` values resolve to the dispatch sub index of
  the label's position; address-taken labels split a block so the value
  is dispatchable.  Weak/static-inline refs resolve to NULL; `__asm__`-
  and `__attribute__((alias))`-renamed symbols resolve to their targets.
- dead regions (NOEVAL statement-expressions) are NOP-filled and their
  loop back-edges are suppressed so the code falls through instead of
  looping.

Remaining failures (both are feature gaps / edge cases, not regressions)

- `96_nodata_wanted` (WRONG, one line): the `&&label` address-difference
  size measurement reports `zero` for the text size — the label values
  are sub indices and the `ts1`/`te1` pair lands in the same dispatch
  sub (the `if (!SKIP) {}` between them folds to no code).  The correct
  fix (splitting at code-only `&&label` refs) conflicts with
  `87_dead_code`'s own address-taken labels in dead/control regions, and
  no sym-table discriminator separates the two cases.
- `73_arm64` (REFUSE): the ARM64 HFA struct-vararg calling convention
  (structs > 4 bytes as varargs, plus the c-runtime's `%hfaNN` printf
  formats) is unimplemented — the backend errors on large struct
  varargs.

Sandbox self-containment
------------------------

The wasm32 target runs inside a wasi sandbox with no libc of its own
(the JS runtime provides the `env.$*` imports), so symbols that would
pull in musl glue are synthesized instead:

- `__syscall_*` references compile to internal stubs returning -1 (no
  env import, no undefined-symbol error).
- the wait family (`wait`, `waitpid`, `wait4`, `waitid`) compile to
  stubs returning -10 (ECHILD — the sandbox has no child processes),
  and the fork ships `include/sys/wait.h` (the wasi sysroot omits it)
  so programs that include it still compile.  The `rusage` parameter is
  an opaque `void*` so `sys/resource.h`'s `#error` gate is not pulled
  in.

History note: the fork's wasm32 backend was rewritten twice (a lost
51-commit state at 110 PASS was recovered from session transcripts to
`99e60d8..372f7d1` on the `mob` branch).  `recovery/` holds the replay
engine and the op log used for that recovery.
